{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013aGBctaUwQW62A7MPZ2519","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have a comprehensive understanding. Let me compile my findings.\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\nI've thoroughly searched the go-unix-utils repository for differential test cases handling error conditions and stderr prefix mismatches. Here are my findings:\n\n### Key Pattern: The Solution Used Throughout the Repository\n\nThe repository has a **consistent pattern** for handling the stderr prefix mismatch between the Go binary (e.g., `\"mkdir: error\"`) and the reference binary (e.g., `\"gmkdir: error\"`):\n\n#### 1. **NormalizeFunc Definition** \nLocation: `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5386/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 26-28)\n\n```go\n// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\ntype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n```\n\n#### 2. **DiffTest Structure with ExitCode and Normalize Fields**\nLocation: Same file (lines 46-55)\n\nThe `DiffTest` struct includes:\n- `ExitCode: int` - Expected non-zero exit code for error tests\n- `Normalize: []NormalizeFunc` - Array of normalization functions applied to both stdout and stderr before comparison\n\n#### 3. **Error Case Test Examples**\n\nMultiple cmd packages use this pattern with non-zero exit codes:\n\n**mkdir** (`/cmd/mkdir/mkdir_test.go`, lines 30-37):\n```go\n{\n    Name:     \"missing_operand\",\n    Args:     []string{},\n    ExitCode: 1,\n    Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{\n        normalizeBinaryName,\n    },\n}\n```\n\n**ln** (`/cmd/ln/ln_test.go`, lines 29-38):\n```go\n{\n    Name:     \"no_args\",\n    Args:     []string{},\n    ExitCode: 1,\n    Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{\n        normalizeBinaryName,\n    },\n}\n```\n\n**unlink** (`/cmd/unlink/unlink_test.go`, lines 52-58):\n```go\n{\n    Name:      \"zero-args\",\n    ExitCode:  1,\n    Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normBinaryName},\n}\n```\n\n#### 4. **Binary Name Normalization Pattern**\n\nThe most common normalization function uses a regex to strip the binary name prefix:\n\n**mkdir** (lines 431-435):\n```go\nvar binaryNameRe = regexp.MustCompile(`(/\\S+/)?g?mkdir\\b`)\n\nfunc normalizeBinaryName(b []byte) []byte {\n    return binaryNameRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"mkdir\"))\n}\n```\n\n**ln** (lines 648-652):\n```go\nvar binaryNameRe = regexp.MustCompile(`(/\\S+/)?g?ln\\b`)\n\nfunc normalizeBinaryName(b []byte) []byte {\n    return binaryNameRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"ln\"))\n}\n```\n\nThe regex pattern `(?:/\\S+/)?g?` matches:\n- Optional path prefix (`/\\S+/`)?\n- Optional leading `g` character (`g?`)\n\n#### 5. **More Complex Normalization Chains**\n\nFor commands like `cut`, `paste`, and `readlink`, multiple normalizers are chained:\n\n**cut** (`/cmd/cut/cut_test.go`, lines 27-37):\n```go\nbinaryNameRe := regexp.MustCompile(`(?:/\\S+/)?g?cut`)\nnormalizeBinaryName := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n    return binaryNameRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"cut\"))\n})\nnormalizeErrCase := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n    return bytes.ToLower(b)\n})\nnormalizeOpenPrefix := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n    return bytes.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\": open \"), []byte(\": \"))\n})\nerrNorm := []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeBinaryName, normalizeErrCase, normalizeOpenPrefix}\n```\n\n**readlink** (`/cmd/readlink/readlink_test.go`, lines 30-35):\n```go\nnormalizeErrors := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n    s := strings.ToLower(string(b))\n    s = strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", \"\")\n    s = strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"`\", \"\")\n    return []byte(s)\n})\n```\n\n**realpath** (`/cmd/realpath/realpath_test.go`, lines 30-34):\n```go\nlowerStderr := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n    return bytes.ToLower(b)\n})\nerrNorm := []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeBinaryName, lowerStderr}\n```\n\n#### 6. **Normalization Application**\n\nLocation: `/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 168-174)\n\n```go\nfunc applyNormalizers(res binaryResult, fns []NormalizeFunc) binaryResult {\n    for _, fn := range fns {\n        res.stdout = fn(res.stdout)\n        res.stderr = fn(res.stderr)\n    }\n    return res\n}\n```\n\nNormalizers are applied to **both stdout and stderr** of both binaries before comparison.\n\n### Files with Error Condition Tests (Non-zero ExitCode)\n\n38 test files with error cases:\n- `/cmd/mkdir/mkdir_test.go`\n- `/cmd/ln/ln_test.go`\n- `/cmd/unlink/unlink_test.go`\n- `/cmd/realpath/realpath_test.go`\n- `/cmd/readlink/readlink_test.go`\n- `/cmd/whoami/whoami_test.go`\n- `/cmd/cut/cut_test.go`\n- `/cmd/paste/paste_test.go`\n- `/cmd/comm/comm_test.go`\n- `/cmd/uniq/uniq_test.go`\n- `/cmd/logname/logname_test.go`\n- `/cmd/groups/groups_test.go`\n- And 26 others\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nThe repository handles stderr prefix mismatches by:\n1. **Not comparing raw stderr** - Instead, applying normalization functions\n2. **Using regex-based stripping** - The pattern `(?:/\\S+/)?g?` strips both path and the `g` prefix\n3. **Chaining normalizers** - For additional variations (case differences, quote styles, etc.)\n4. **Normalizing both binaries** - Same functions applied to Go and reference binary outputs\n\nThis allows tests to verify behavior parity without caring about the binary name differences between `mkdir` and `gmkdir`, or `tr` and `gtr`."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2c6b651d-10da-4679-b0c4-38e48c4ea296","uuid":"a62c32dc-6d8c-4aac-b080-c8ddce7148dd","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:37:03.846Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the go-unix-utils repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5386, search for existing differential test cases that test error conditions (non-zero exit code). I need to understand how other cmd/